### Tested versions

4.5.beta4

### System information

Godot v4.5.beta4 - Ubuntu 24.04.2 LTS 24.04 on X11 - X11 display driver, Multi-window, 2 monitors - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 (nvidia; 570.169) - AMD Ryzen 5 2600 Six-Core Processor (12 threads) - 62.72 GiB memory

### Issue description

Recent Blender versions now allow you to export geometry nodes objects directly into a gltf file with the `Geometry nodes instances` and `GPU instances` export options. So I can design my entire level in Blender, including scattering decoration, and import it into Godot.
But when I import this file into Godot, instead of using one multimesh, it creates a separate mesh for every object, making the scene incredibly GPU inefficient.

### Steps to reproduce

Below is an example gltf file exported from Blender. A single cube scattered into nine cubes on a grid in geometry nodes. Notice how every `node` refers to the same `mesh`. One would assume this would trigger a multimesh in Godot. But if you import this file in Godot into a new inherited scene each node becomes its own mesh.

